156
00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:39.040
Adwait Mane: So, outline of the talk, we'll start with an overview of modeling and system identification, a little bit of mathematical background.

157
00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:47.820
Adwait Mane: Then we'll get into viscoelastic models, and then transfer functions of those models. Finally, we'll end with

158
00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:53.650
Adwait Mane: Ways we can use the viscoelastic models of tendons and muscles.

159
00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.799
Adwait Mane: So first is the overview.

160
00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:04.520
Adwait Mane: There are two broad categories of modeling paradigms. One is…

161
00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:12.859
Adwait Mane: Deriving models from first principles, so that's when we derive governing equations from the fundamental laws of physics.

162
00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.899
Adwait Mane: Such as Newton's laws of motion.

163
00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:21.640
Adwait Mane: Or laws of thermodynamics, or electromagnetism and such.

164
00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:31.299
Adwait Mane: And another category is system identification, where we rely heavily on using experimental input-output data.

165
00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:45.090
Adwait Mane: So, system identification, a useful definition, is the process of building mathematical models of dynamical systems based on experimentally measured data from the system.

166
00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:56.619
Adwait Mane: So, and system identification models can be used to analyze or understand the behavior of a system.

167
00:21:57.010 --> 00:22:01.510
Adwait Mane: They can also be used to predict the output of a system from the input.

168
00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:10.449
Adwait Mane: And, they can be used to derive a control system or a controller, to interact with the system.

169
00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:20.439
Adwait Mane: So this is an example of system identification in biology. We have a fish here, in brown, inside a tube, which has a pattern on the side.

170
00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:22.130
Adwait Mane: This is the top view.

171
00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:29.970
Adwait Mane: So, R and X are the longitudinal position of the tube and the fish.

172
00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:38.990
Adwait Mane: And these are defined as the input and the output. So, the tube position is the input, fish position is the output.

173
00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:52.380
Adwait Mane: And then an oscillatory input is provided to the tube, and the fish tries to maintain a constant offset E.

174
00:22:54.050 --> 00:23:01.070
Adwait Mane: So, that's the system. And the authors in this study, they used system identification to…

175
00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:12.289
Adwait Mane: Identify the input-output transfer function, For this combined controller and locomotion dynamics system, so…

176
00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:20.519
Adwait Mane: the input is R, the output is, is X in this case.

177
00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:24.419
Adwait Mane: So these are the kinds of systems that can be modeled.

178
00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:28.750
Adwait Mane: And this extends to a variety of domains.

179
00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:38.559
Adwait Mane: So these modeling paradigms that I mentioned, in practice, we usually, use a blend of these two.

180
00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:44.039
Adwait Mane: Which gives us 3 broad categories of system identification models.

181
00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:47.059
Adwait Mane: White box, gray box, and black box.

182
00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:56.719
Adwait Mane: White box models mean they have a high structure and fidelity, which means the governing equations for the system are well established.

183
00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:08.460
Adwait Mane: And, all the parameters have a physical interpretation, and the system identification is used primarily to determine those physical parameters on the system.

184
00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.720
Adwait Mane: The unknown physical parameters of the system.

185
00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:21.029
Adwait Mane: And gray and black box models, rely less on the governing equations and more on the external… on the experimental input-output data.

186
00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:31.040
Adwait Mane: And, okay, I wanna mention some terminology here.

187
00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:39.210
Adwait Mane: There are broad categories of… Dynamical… dynamic or dynamical system, and static systems.

188
00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:48.410
Adwait Mane: These terms are used in a variety of domains that extend beyond physics and the sciences.

189
00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:56.529
Adwait Mane: So, a dynamic system is one whose state evolves over time, and a static system is one that is at equilibrium.

190
00:24:56.750 --> 00:25:03.340
Adwait Mane: So, example of… Domains that use

191
00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:06.769
